module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.sibling_operand_effect_order_is_underdetermined_and_unenforced

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

// THIS ROW IS A DERIVATION READ, NOT A REPAIR, AND IT DELIBERATELY PROPOSES NO NEW VOCABULARY.
// The question it was opened to answer is whether sibling-operand order is UNDERIVABLE or merely
// UNDECLARED, because the two have completely different remedies. The answer it reaches is a third
// thing that neither option named: the deciding fact is ALREADY DECLARED, and what it declares is
// that the order is ABSENT. So the remedy is not to declare an order -- it is to refuse the program
// whose meaning depends on the order the model declines to carry. Adding a sequencing fact to the
// closed vocabulary of DESIGN section 4 to preserve those programs would be the larger change AND
// the wrong one, and for named siblings it would contradict content identity outright.
data sibling_operand_effect_order_is_underdetermined_and_unenforced: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "sibling_operand_effect_order_is_underdetermined_and_unenforced" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**sibling-operand effect order is underdetermined by the model, and the absence is declared but unenforced** (INVALID STATE: an accepted program in which two siblings of one operand list each reach an observable effect, so its meaning depends on which runs first, while every authority the realizations consult declines to carry that order -- and nothing refuses the program).",

    "THE QUESTION ASKED FIRST, BECAUSE IT DECIDES EVERYTHING DOWNSTREAM: is the order UNDERIVABLE, or merely UNDECLARED? Re-derived over the slice source -> lowering -> Node -> each realization, the answer is BOTH HALVES AND NEITHER OPTION. Between two effectful siblings the order is underivable, because there is no dependency edge to derive it from. But the fact that decides what to do about that is already declared, and it declares the order absent rather than missing. Four facts settle it and none of them is new.",

    "FACT A -- SEQUENCING IS ALREADY IN THE CLOSED VOCABULARY, CARRIED PER BEHAVIOR AND NAMING ITS PAIR. Bind is not a general sequencer; it sequences exactly one pair. v2.std.node behavior_edges_conform admits Bind at exactly three positional children, and v2.compiler.eval eval_bind_node_eval evaluates the value edge, extends the environment through interpretation.bind, and only then evaluates the body -- so value-before-body is fixed by the Bind interpreter contract and NOT by any dataflow edge between those two children. Branch fixes condition-before-the-chosen-arm the same way, Match fixes scrutinee-before-an-arm. A let SEQUENCE is nested Binds, so consecutive statements are not siblings at all: they stand in a containment relation and their order is derived from the nesting. The audit finding that opened this lane grouped let sequences with argument lists; that part does not survive the read, and separating them is what makes the remedy small.",

    "FACT B -- THE GAP IS ONE BEHAVIOR WIDE, AND THE STRUCTURE IT LACKS A MEANING FOR IS ALREADY THERE. Transform is the only behavior with N unconstrained siblings and no such pair contract, and Transform is what a call is: v2.std.node_query node_is_call answers on a ComputationNode Transform whose positional child 0 is the callee, and call_argument_targets is the tail of that same positional list. So the model DOES carry a total order over those children, and positional index is ALREADY load-bearing semantically -- index 0 is the callee here, index 0 is the scrutinee under Match, the three Bind positions are binder, value and body. What is missing is not the sequence. It is any row saying that this particular sequence means DEMAND ORDER.",

    "FACT C -- AND THE CORPUS HAS ALREADY RULED THAT IT MUST NOT BE GIVEN THAT MEANING. gunbc.host_effect_realize srv3_chown_then_read_back carries the ruling on its own carrier: the mutation is sequenced by a let inside the arm, NOT by argument position, because implicit evaluation order is not a sequencing authority and the fact that eagerness happened to be what the author wanted that time does not make it one (side-chat review of #8760). It is recorded there because an earlier revision of that same function passed a privileged chown as an ARGUMENT and defended it in a comment as deliberately load-bearing eagerness. That is this class, found in review, in production code, and repaired by hand into the behavior that declares the order.",

    "FACT D -- FOR NAMED SIBLINGS THE ORDER IS NOT MERELY UNDERIVABLE, IT IS UNREPRESENTABLE, WHICH IS WHY DECLARING ONE IS NOT AVAILABLE AS AN OPTION. v2.std.node connective_edge_discipline gives Conj and Disj the LabeledEdges discipline -- so every record and every coproduct -- and canonicalize_labeled_for_content_hash SORTS labeled children by label before hashing them. Two spellings differing only in the textual order of their named siblings are therefore THE SAME NODE under v2.std.node content_hash. A declared order over named siblings would be a semantic fact the identity relation cannot see: one node, two behaviors, which is the meaning fork DESIGN section 3 forbids. The identity authority has already answered this question, in the direction of absence.",

    "SO THE GAP IS AN UNENFORCED DECLARATION AND NOT A MISSING ONE. Of the three honest possibilities -- declare the order, refuse the program as ambiguous, declare the order unobservable and enforce that -- the first is refuted by FACT C and FACT D, and the second and third are ONE repair rather than two: the order is already declared unobservable, and enforcing it IS refusing the program that observes it. The author's remedy is the rewrite #8760 performed by hand, into the let that already declares what it needs.",

    "WHY AN ORACLE CANNOT SETTLE THIS ONE, re-derived rather than accepted, and it holds. gunbc.guarantee_stall.authority_target_same_expression_equivalence_stall names as its trigger a lane that runs ONE expression through BOTH realizations and refuses a divergence. On the STRICTNESS axis that lane has an authority to appeal to once std.operator_realization OperandDemand lands. On the ORDER axis it has none: if the interpreter's child fold and an emitted target disagree about which of two sibling effects happened first, NO ROW SAYS WHICH IS RIGHT, so the lane can report the disagreement and cannot adjudicate it. A differential oracle over an undeclared fact is a change detector between two arms, not a check. This class needs the refusal first and the comparison never.",

    "WHY IT IS NOT THE TWO ROWS NEAREST TO IT, checked rather than assumed. It is not the operand-DEMAND fact: std.operator_realization OperandDemand answers WHICH operands an operator asks for, derived from std.logic's own match, and says nothing about the order of two operands BOTH of which are demanded -- demand may not stand in for order, and a reader who lets it will read a landed demand row as coverage of this. It is not `realization_arms_diverge_on_whether_the_program_refuses`: that row's invalid state is a connective whose operand evaluation STRATEGY is unmodeled, strict in one arm and lazy in another. Here both siblings are demanded by every arm and the arms may still differ in which effect happened first, with no refusal anywhere and every value-comparison oracle green. Same neighbourhood, different invalid state, and the remedies point opposite ways: that row's trigger asks for a fact to be DECLARED, this row's asks for a program to be REFUSED.",

    "A GRAIN MISMATCH WORTH NAMING ON THE SIBLING ROW RATHER THAN HERE, because DESIGN section 4b(3) makes a trigger that names less than the capability it restores the whole defect: `realization_arms_diverge_on_whether_the_program_refuses` states its next-rung trigger as EVALUATION ORDER MODELED AS A PROPERTY OF A CONNECTIVE AND CONSULTED BY BOTH REALIZATIONS. A landed demand row over the binary connectives satisfies the sentence and leaves sibling ORDER exactly where it is. The trigger's grain is a connective and a strategy; this class's grain is N siblings of one node and their sequence.",

    "WHAT THE ARMS DO TODAY, AND THE CLAIM THIS RECEIPT WITHDRAWS. An earlier cut of this row said the three arms AGREE and that the agreement was a property of the targets rather than of any declared row. THE AGREEMENT HALF IS FALSE FOR NAMED ARGUMENTS and is withdrawn here rather than left standing, because a row claiming a latent gap where a LIVE DIVERGENCE exists is the rung inflation DESIGN section 4b(1) names, applied to a class's own description. What survives is the second half: no arm does what it does because a row told it to. For POSITIONAL children the three arms do coincide -- v2.compiler.eval eval_fold_tree folds n.children with fold_list in stored edge order, v1_compiler.interpreter eval_expr_inner evaluates both binop operands in source order, and Rust fixes argument evaluation left to right -- and that coincidence is still not evidence.",

    "THE FIRST LIVE DIVERGENCE, RE-DERIVED FROM THE SYMBOLS RATHER THAN ACCEPTED, AND IT IS THE STRONGEST SPECIMEN THIS ROW HAS: TWO ROUTES THAT ALREADY EXIST DISAGREE ON SIBLING EFFECT ORDER TODAY. v1.compiler.emit order_typed_call_args_from_semantics folds over the ResolvedDirectCallSemantics application_plan and selects args by matched_argument_index, so the emitted Rust argument list is in FORMAL -- declaration -- order whatever the call site spelled; Rust then evaluates that list left to right. v1_compiler.interpreter eval_call maps over node.children, which is CALL-SITE order, evaluating each argument as it goes. So for `fn f(a, b)` called as f with b bound to a write and a bound to a read, the interpreter performs write-then-read and the emitted binary performs read-then-write. Both routes ship. Neither is wrong, because NO ROW SAYS WHICH IS RIGHT -- which is exactly the state the lane predicted would make a differential oracle useless, now found rather than forecast. Read from another lane (smart-swift-440, relayed by snappy-deer-443) and verified here against both symbols.",

    "THE SECOND LIVE DIVERGENCE, AND IT IS IN A RECORD LITERAL RATHER THAN AN ARGUMENT LIST, so it is the labeled-sibling face of the same fact. v1.compiler.emit_rust lowers a FreeMonoid Cons literal to a block that clones the TAIL and then inserts the HEAD into it -- `let mut __cons_v = (*<tail>).clone(); __cons_v.insert(0, <head>); __cons_v` -- so the tail expression is evaluated before the head expression whatever order the two fields were written in, while the interpreter evaluates the literal's children in stored order. Two effect-reaching fields of one Cons therefore run in opposite orders on the two routes. It matters beyond its own shape because it shows the divergence does not need a declaration to reorder against: an emitter lowering that HOISTS one sibling is enough, which this row already named as a realization that could violate the order and can now name as one that does.",

    "WHAT THE TWO SPECIMENS CHANGE ABOUT THE REMEDY: NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE POINT WORTH RECORDING. They do not argue for declaring an order -- FACT C and FACT D still refuse that, and a declared order would now have to adjudicate BETWEEN TWO SHIPPING ROUTES, which is a migration and not a modelling decision. They raise the urgency and they supply the wall its red: the named-argument reorder is authorable as a fixture program whose two routes disagree observably, so the refusal has a discriminating red that is a property of the CORPUS rather than of a planted shape. And they retire the softest sentence this row carried, that the missing authority costs nothing until the day it costs everything. The day is in the past tense.",

    "THE EXECUTION STATUS OF THE TWO SPECIMENS, STATED BECAUSE A READ IS NOT A RUN AND DESIGN SECTION 5 TREATS THE DIFFERENCE AS THE WHOLE QUESTION. Both are STRUCTURAL READS of the two emitters and the interpreter -- order_typed_call_args_from_semantics selecting by matched_argument_index over the application_plan, eval_call mapping over node.children, and the Cons lowering cloning the tail before inserting the head. NEITHER HAS BEEN RUN. No fixture has yet been put through both routes and observed to disagree, so what is established is that the two routes are SPECIFIED to order siblings differently, not that a program has been seen to. That gap is not a caveat to be carried indefinitely: running the named-argument specimen through both routes and observing the disagreement IS the discriminating red the wall owes, so the wall PR closes this sentence rather than repeating it. Until it does, a reader may cite these specimens as a reading of the emitters and may not cite them as a measured divergence.",

    "AND THE MECHANISM UNDERNEATH THE FIRST SPECIMEN IS A DIFFERENT DEFECT THAT THIS ROW SHOULD NAME RATHER THAN ABSORB, flagged by the same lane and re-derived here: v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold body_lower_transform_call builds the call node as an operator edge followed by one Edge per argument labelled Positional, so THE ARGUMENT NAME IS NOT CARRIED INTO THE LOWERED NODE AT ALL. The consequence for this class is direct. Positional index in a v2-lowered call is doing TWO jobs at once -- which formal parameter an argument feeds, and the sequence the children stand in -- while v1.compiler.emit reads the binding from names through the application_plan and is therefore free to permute the sequence. One question, which argument feeds which parameter, answered by two authorities that disagree about whether sequence is part of the answer: that is the DESIGN section 3 fork, and on this route it is not a consequence of the missing order fact but its CAUSE. FACT B of this row said positional index is already load-bearing semantically and treated that as the structure an order could attach to; this says the index is already OVERLOADED, which is why attaching an order to it would be worse than it looked. NOTHING HERE PROPOSES AN EDIT TO THE LOWERING FOLD: it is a pipeline stage DESIGN names as load-bearing, the finding is an unexecuted read, and it is recorded so the wall author knows the sequence they would refuse over is not a clean carrier.",

    "THE REALIZATIONS THAT COULD VIOLATE IT, NAMED RATHER THAN IMPLIED, which is what the read owes if the answer is a wall: an emission target whose own specification leaves argument evaluation unsequenced -- C is modeled in this repository at v2.extdeps.languages.c and is exactly such a language; the other two body emitters, v1.compiler.emit_python and v1.compiler.emit_go; any emitter change that hoists an operand into a temporary, which is invisible to content identity for labeled siblings by FACT D and invisible to every value-comparison oracle by construction; and any evaluator that folds children in other than stored order, which is a one-line change in eval_fold_tree away. TWO OF THESE ARE NO LONGER HYPOTHETICAL AND A FOURTH MECHANISM WAS NOT ANTICIPATED HERE AT ALL: the hoist-into-a-temporary case is realized by the FreeMonoid Cons lowering, and an emitter REORDERING named arguments into declaration order -- which this list did not imagine, because it assumed a realization would preserve the authored sequence or hoist out of it, not permute it -- is realized by order_typed_call_args_from_semantics. Both are recorded as specimens above, and the list is left as written rather than rewritten so the miss stays visible.",

    "HARM: two realizations of one accepted program differ in which effect happened first. Against a source-to-emission path that is silent wrongness, which DESIGN section 4b places outside the ladder rather than on a rung. The harm is not the unusual case -- a read that observes another sibling's write, a chown that lands after the read-back that was supposed to check it, two appends in the order the reader did not intend -- it is that the program was ACCEPTED while its meaning was never fixed, so no arm is wrong and there is nothing to appeal to.",

    "THE DISCRIMINATING RED IS AUTHORABLE AND IS NOT YET AUTHORED, which is the question DESIGN section 4b says to ask BEFORE writing the check. The forbidden state is expressible as source handed to the compiler by a fixture, which is the shape the effectful-item witnesses already use -- dag/test/claim/effectful_item_kind_collapse_witness_test carries a function declaring a resource through a uses row and calling it from inside an infix expression, as a source string. Two effect-reaching siblings in one operand list are writable today and nothing refuses them, so the red for the proposed wall is authorable at exactly the boundary a compiler's regression probes live at, and declining to enrol it would be the specification-without-execution DESIGN section 5 forbids.",

    "THE POPULATION IS UNMEASURED AND IS NOT ZERO, and the instrument is named rather than a figure transcribed. #8760 is one instance, caught in review and rewritten by hand; the class is survivorship-filtered in the same way its neighbour records, because an author whose two argument effects visibly raced rewrote them while authoring. What would count it is a lens over lowered bodies answering, per Transform node, how many of its argument children reach a declared effect -- which is the SAME call-reachability capability v2.std.effect_reach names as the trigger retiring its own ceiling row. The two share one trigger, and neither should be priced as though it had its own.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder on the source-to-emission path, and mitigatable only where one arm refuses loudly enough to be noticed. CEILING: structurally impossible, and that is derived rather than aspirational -- the predicate is decidable, because how many argument children of one Transform node reach a declared effect is a call-reachability question over declared uses requirements inside a closed closure; the fact the wall needs is already declared, by FACT A and FACT C; and what is missing is only the refusal. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS A CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: acceptance refuses a node whose sibling operand list carries more than one effect-reaching child, so that the only way to write two ordered effects is through the behavior that declares their order -- sufficient for the meaning of every accepted program to be independent of the order no row carries. A lens that COUNTS the sites contributes to that capability and does not retire this row.",

    "THE CLIMB IS TWO RUNGS AND THE TRIGGER BELONGS TO THE SECOND, decided by the owning lane (snappy-deer-443, 2026-09-22) after this read: THE NARROWER WALL GOES FIRST. Its predicate is the DECLARED uses requirement of each sibling child -- a fact the corpus already carries per function and which needs no reachability analysis to read -- so acceptance refuses a Transform node more than one of whose sibling operand children names a function declaring a uses requirement. That is decidable today and its discriminating red is authorable today as fixture source, which is the whole reason it is first. It is a NARROWER predicate and not a weaker one: it misses a sibling that reaches an effect THROUGH a call chain rather than declaring one itself, so it is structural over the population it covers and silent over the rest. THE TRIGGER THEREFORE STAYS NAMED AT THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AT THIS ARTIFACT, as DESIGN section 4b(3) requires: what climbs the remaining population is call-reachability over declared uses requirements inside a closed closure -- the capability v2.std.effect_reach already names as the trigger retiring its own ceiling row -- and landing the uses-requirement wall SATISFIES NEITHER THAT TRIGGER NOR RETIRES THIS ROW. A trigger naming the narrower wall would be satisfied while the capability it restores stayed dead, which is the grain mismatch this row already names against its neighbour.",

    "AND THE WALL IS ADMITTED ONLY BEHIND A CENSUS, which is the same discipline and not extra ceremony: before the refusal is enrolled, the population of ACCEPTED nodes on main carrying more than one uses-declaring child in one sibling operand list is counted as a claim row rather than as prose, and every hit is dispositioned as either this class -- repaired by hand into the let, which is what #8760 did -- or a false positive of the predicate. A refusal enrolled without that count cannot distinguish a wall that fires on nothing from a wall that breaks the corpus, and the count is the only thing that says which. The wall lands with the census, a red fixture program that is refused, and a positive control in which the let-rewritten form is ACCEPTED -- the shape dag/test/claim/effectful_item_kind_collapse_witness_test already uses -- because a refusal without the accepting control does not establish that the remedy it names is writable.",

    "WHAT THIS ROW DOES NOT PROPOSE, stated because it is the move a reader will reach for first: declaring an argument-order fact. That would add a sequencing primitive to a closed vocabulary in order to preserve programs the corpus has already ruled should be rewritten, it would be unrepresentable for named siblings under the identity relation, and it would make the order of a record's fields load-bearing where content identity says it is not. If order is ever declared anyway, the modelling standard to match is v2.compiler.effect_demand, which puts the EXECUTION ROUTE IN THE IDENTITY KEY, so a claim about one route cannot be spent as coverage of another by construction.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, stated to generalise past operand lists: when two realizations of one construct must agree on WHEN something happens, ask which authority carries the order, and then ask whether that authority carries it as a PRESENCE or as an ABSENCE. An absence is a perfectly good answer and is often the right one -- but an absence that nothing enforces is indistinguishable from a fact nobody has got round to declaring, and the two have opposite remedies. The tell is a construct whose realizations agree today and whose agreement no row explains.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
